# Base torrc baked into the Tor MikroTik container image.
#
# This file is intentionally minimal. tor.sh generates a runtime override at
# /var/lib/tor/torrc.runtime from the container's environment variables (ports,
# VirtualAddrNetwork, exit nodes, etc.) and this file %includes it, so the
# effective config = these defaults + env-driven values (last line wins).
#
# TRANSPARENT-PROXY MODEL (why these options):
#   TransPort  — accepts plain TCP connections that the router dst-nats here and
#                sends them through the Tor network. Clients need no proxy config.
#   DNSPort    — resolves names via Tor. For .onion (and .exit) it returns a
#                synthetic IP from VirtualAddrNetworkIPv4 (default 10.192.0.0/10).
#   AutomapHostsOnResolve + AutomapHostsSuffixes — makes the DNSPort hand out
#                those synthetic IPs for .onion/.exit so TransPort can then carry
#                the TCP flow to the real hidden service.
#
# The router (see yt/bgp-setup/tor-setup) forwards the .onion DNS zone to this
# DNSPort and routes VirtualAddrNetwork -> this container + dst-nats TCP to
# TransPort. Without those two data-plane pieces, resolving works but packets
# to the synthetic IPs go nowhere.

# Run as the unprivileged tor user (Debian packages it as debian-tor).
User debian-tor

# Keep logs on stdout so `/container` logging=yes surfaces them in RouterOS log.
Log notice stdout

# We are a CLIENT only. Never act as a relay or exit for others.
SocksPolicy accept *
ExitRelay 0

# Reasonable client hardening: don't run any server-side features.
ClientOnly 1

# Pull in the env-generated runtime settings (ports, virtual net, exit nodes).
# %include tolerates the file not existing on the very first parse; tor.sh
# always writes it before launching tor, so in practice it is present.
%include /var/lib/tor/torrc.runtime
